---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Breadcrumb from "@/components/Breadcrumb.astro";
import Main from "@/components/Main.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import config from "@/config";
---

<Layout title={`Privacy | ${config.site.title}`}>
  <Header />
  <Breadcrumb />
  <Main pageTitle="Privacy Policy" class="app-prose">
    <p>
      Daniel Newton ('I', 'me', 'my') operates the website
      https://www.danielnewton.dev. This privacy policy describes how I handle
      personal data when you use my website.
    </p>

    <h2>Data Controller</h2>
    <p>
      Daniel Newton is the Data Controller for what is described in this Privacy
      Policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Data Collection</h2>
    <p>
      I use the following service providers to process information when you
      visit this website or contact me.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>PostHog</strong>: website analytics and error tracking. PostHog
        only starts after you choose Accept on the banner. It then records which
        pages you view, how you interact with them (such as clicks), and
        information about your browser and device. If a script on the page
        fails, it also records the error message, where in the code it happened
        and the page you were on. Session recording, heatmaps, performance
        measurement and surveys are switched off in the site's code. Your IP
        address is visible to Cloudflare (see below) as it handles the
        connection. PostHog uses it to estimate your approximate location and
        then discards it, so it isn't stored with the analytics data. The data
        goes to PostHog's US servers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vercel</strong>: hosting, and website performance measurement
        with Vercel Speed Insights. As my host, Vercel receives each request to
        the site, including your IP address, the page requested and browser
        details. Speed Insights reports page load performance together with the
        page address, browser, device type and operating system, network speed
        and country. Vercel describes this data as anonymous and says it can't
        be used to follow a visit across pages. Hosting and Speed Insights run
        whatever you choose on the banner.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Proton</strong>: email. If you email me, Proton processes the
        message so that I can read and reply to it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      PostHog analytics goes through PostHog's managed proxy on my own domain.
      Cloudflare processes that traffic on PostHog's behalf to provide the
      proxy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each provider handles data under its own terms: <a
        href="https://posthog.com/privacy">PostHog privacy policy</a
      >
      (see also PostHog's <a href="https://posthog.com/dpa"
        >data processing agreement</a
      >, <a href="https://posthog.com/subprocessors">subprocessor list</a> and
      <a href="https://posthog.com/docs/advanced/proxy"
        >managed proxy documentation</a
      >),
      <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy">Vercel privacy policy</a
      >
      and
      <a href="https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/privacy-policy"
        >Speed Insights privacy information</a
      >, and
      <a href="https://proton.me/legal/privacy">Proton privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Legal Basis for Processing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Consent: I use PostHog only if you accept it on the analytics banner.
        You can withdraw consent at any time (see Your Rights below).
        Withdrawing doesn't affect processing that happened before you withdrew.
      </li>
      <li>
        Legitimate interest: delivering the website and keeping it working and
        fast (Vercel hosting and Speed Insights), and reading and replying to
        emails you send me (Proton).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Data Transfer</h2>
    <p>
      Some of this processing happens outside the European Economic Area (EEA).
      PostHog data goes to PostHog's US servers, and Vercel and Proton may also
      process data outside the EEA.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because analytics traffic passes through Cloudflare, Cloudflare may
      process it in data centers in several countries, including outside the
      EEA, before it reaches PostHog. PostHog doesn't guarantee that this
      traffic stays in the EU, even for its EU region.
    </p>
    <p>
      Transfers are covered by the mechanisms the providers document. Vercel's <a
        href="https://vercel.com/legal/dpa">data processing addendum</a
      > describes the EU Standard Contractual Clauses, and its privacy policy says
      it complies with the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework. PostHog publishes a
      <a href="https://posthog.com/dpa">data processing agreement</a> and a
      <a href="https://posthog.com/subprocessors">subprocessor list</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Retention Period</h2>
    <p>
      PostHog keeps analytics data for 12 months. It deletes older data in a
      monthly cleanup, so some of it can last up to about a month longer. I may
      delete it sooner, for example because you ask me to. Vercel and Proton
      keep data according to their own policies. Your Accept or Reject choice
      stays in your browser until you change it or clear your site data.
    </p>
    <p>
      To have your PostHog data deleted, contact me. It is linked to a random
      identifier stored in your browser rather than your name, so I may need
      that identifier to find it.
    </p>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>
      The security of your personal information is important to me. I implement
      the following security measures:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Data encryption via HTTPS/TLS</li>
      <li>Limited access controls</li>
      <li>Regular security updates</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      However, remember that no method of transmission over the Internet, or
      method of electronic storage, is 100% secure. While I strive to use
      commercially acceptable means to protect your personal information, I
      cannot guarantee its absolute security.
    </p>

    <h2>Your Rights</h2>
    <p>
      If the GDPR or a similar law applies to you, you may have the following
      rights. Which ones apply depends on where you live and on the legal basis
      for the processing.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Right to information about the processing of your data</li>
      <li>Right to access your data</li>
      <li>Right to rectification of incorrect data</li>
      <li>Right to erasure ('right to be forgotten')</li>
      <li>Right to restriction of processing</li>
      <li>Right to data portability</li>
      <li>Right to object to processing</li>
      <li>Right not to be subject to automated decision-making</li>
      <li>Right to complain to your local data protection authority</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To withdraw your consent, choose "Privacy settings" in the footer of any
      page and then Reject. PostHog stops collecting new data from your browser
      and the site removes the PostHog entries it stored there. Withdrawing
      doesn't delete data PostHog already collected. To have that deleted,
      contact me.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies and local storage</h2>
    <p>
      The site stores the following in your browser. "Privacy settings" in the
      footer lets you change your choice at any time.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your analytics choice</strong> (local storage, key <code
          >analytics_consent</code
        >): remembers whether you accepted or rejected PostHog, on this browser
        only. It holds no identifier. It is set when you choose Accept or Reject
        and is kept until you change it or clear your site data. Deleting
        cookies alone doesn't remove it, because it isn't a cookie. Clearing
        local storage does, and then I will ask you again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>PostHog</strong> (cookie and local storage, names starting with <code
          >ph_</code
        >): only after you accept. Holds a random identifier for your browser
        and information PostHog uses to group your page views into a visit. If
        you reject or withdraw, or have not accepted, the site removes any such
        entries it finds. If your browser blocks local storage, I can't remember
        a choice, so PostHog stays off.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Theme</strong> (local storage, key <code>theme</code>): set only
        if you use the light/dark toggle, to remember your selection.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Vercel hosting and Speed Insights aren't controlled by your analytics
      choice. The Speed Insights script currently stores nothing in your
      browser.
    </p>
    <p>
      Earlier versions of the banner didn't name PostHog or say what it records.
      If you accepted then, I don't treat that as consent and you will be asked
      again. If you rejected then, that choice still applies.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this Privacy Policy</h2>
    <p>
      This Privacy Policy is effective as of <time datetime="2026-11-15"
        >Nov 15, 2026</time
      > and will remain in effect except with respect to any changes in its provisions
      in the future, which will be in effect immediately after being posted on this
      page.
    </p>
    <p>
      I reserve the right to update or change this Privacy Policy at any time
      and you should check this Privacy Policy periodically.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      For questions about this privacy policy or your data, please send me an
      email at <a href="mailto:privacy@danielnewton.dev"
        >privacy@danielnewton.dev</a
      >.
    </p>
  </Main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
